        "notes": "MATH FINDING (report, not fixed) - and it RESOLVES the inconsistency I flagged on thm:groebner-shirshov-energy earlier in this batch. The theorem defines E(xi) = sum_w |c_w(q)| * l(w), with an explicit word-length weight. This proof's Part 3 computes the change as |c(q-q^{-1})| + |c| - |c| = |c|*|q-q^{-1}|, which drops l(w) entirely - it is the change in sum_w |c_w|, the coefficient l^1 norm, not in E. Carrying the length weights instead: the Hecke step sends c.w of length l to c(q-q^{-1}).w' of length l-1 plus c.w'' of length l-2, so Delta E = |c|[(q-q^{-1})(l-1) - 2]. At the proton pin (q - q^{-1} = 0.2090602987956394) that is NEGATIVE for every l below 10.5666 - e.g. -1.791|c| at l = 2 - so the theorem's clause 'Every Hecke step increases the total weight' is false under its own stated E and true under the functional the proof actually uses. Note also that Part 1's 'changes the word length (from l to l-1)' is incomplete: the step yields two terms, of lengths l-1 AND l-2, as Part 3 itself then states. The clean repair is to drop the l(w) factor from the theorem's definition of E, which makes theorem, proof and monotonicity claim all consistent; the alternative (keep l(w)) requires rewriting Part 3 and abandoning the monotonicity clause."
